        // State C (y[2]=1): w=0 -> E (y[4]=1), w=1 -> D (y[3]=1)
        // State D (y[3]=1): w=0 -> F (y[5]=1), w=1 -> A (y[0]=1)
        // State E (y[4]=1): w=0 -> E (y[4]=1), w=1 -> D (y[3]=1)
        // State F (y[5]=1): w=0 -> C (y[2]=1), w=1 -> D (y[3]=1)
        //
        // Next-state signals Y1 and Y3 correspond to y[1] (State B) and y[3] (State D).
        // Let's derive expected Y1 (State B next) and Y3 (State D next):
        // B is next state when:
        // - In A and w=0 -> y[0] & ~w
        //
